630859375, -0.0260009765625, -0.0132904052734375, 0.018280029296875, -0.0129852294921875, -0.0003075599670410156, -0.0086212158203125, 0.0215911865234375, 0.0097808837890625, 0.0193328857421875, -0.0467529296875, -0.013519287109375, -0.0290985107421875, -0.0006766319274902344, 0.00811004638671875, -0.0018777847290039062, 0.0206146240234375, -0.02276611328125, -0.005603790283203125, 0.0059356689453125, -0.0157012939453125, 0.0063934326171875, -0.029296875, -0.0264739990234375, 0.01275634765625, 0.0238037109375, -0.00708770751953125, -0.01189422607421875, 0.0149688720703125, -0.01377105712890625, 0.0171356201171875, -0.010162353515625, -0.0222015380859375, -0.02630615234375, 0.012176513671875, 0.0186920166015625, -0.0143890380859375, 0.0008492469787597656, 0.0164947509765625, -0.00884246826171875, 0.028533935546875, 0.00021708011627197266, 0.01346588134765625, 0.020538330078125, -0.01509857177734375, 0.0267486572265625, -0.00545501708984375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a hallmark of oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, reliably identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains a significant challenge. To address this, we introduce a novel framework for robustly quantifying and distinguishing local spatial phase relationships in oscillator networks, particularly during nonstationary or partially synchronized states where traditional global or amplitude-based measures may fail. Our method leverages spatial ordinal patterns to capture local phase structure, enabling sensitive detection of complex and transient spatiotemporal organization. We demonstrate its effectiveness on both synthetic oscillator networks and human EEG data, and benchmark it against established global and amplitude-based measures.\n\n**Methods**\n\nOur framework is based on spatial ordinal patterns that encode the local ordering of oscillator phases. We focus primarily on one-dimensional (1D) networks (e.g., rings, linear arrays), but the approach generalizes to higher dimensions by appropriately defining neighborhoods (e.g., nearest neighbors in 2D grids or based on adjacency in arbitrary graphs). For each oscillator \\(i\\) with phase \\(\\phi_i\\), we compare its phase to those of its immediate neighbors. In 1D, these are the left (\\(\\phi_{i-1}\\)) and right (\\(\\phi_{i+1}\\)) neighbors; in 2D or irregular topologies, neighbors are defined by network connectivity (see Supplement for details).\n\nThe encoding proceeds as follows: for each neighbor \\(j\\) of oscillator \\(i\\), we compute the phase difference \\(\\Delta\\phi_{ij} = \\phi_i - \\phi_j\\). If \\(|\\Delta\\phi_{ij}| < \\epsilon\\) (where \\(\\epsilon\\) is a small threshold), we assign a tie symbol \u20180\u2019; if \\(\\Delta\\phi_{ij} > \\epsilon\\), we assign \u2018+\u2019; if \\(\\Delta\\phi_{ij} < -\\epsilon\\), we assign \u2018\u2212\u2019. For example, if an oscillator\u2019s phase is higher than its left neighbor but equal (within \\(\\epsilon\\)) to its right neighbor, its pattern is encoded as (\u2018+\u2019, \u20180\u2019). This stepwise encoding ensures that near-equal phases are not misclassified due to noise or discretization.\n\nThe threshold \\(\\epsilon\\) is chosen to balance sensitivity to small phase differences and robustness to noise. We set \\(\\epsilon\\) to 5% of the full phase range (i.e., \\(0.05 \\times 2\\pi\\)), based on preliminary analysis of phase noise distributions in both synthetic and EEG data. We confirmed that results are robust for \\(\\epsilon\\) between 2\u201310% of the phase range (see Supplement, Section 2), and recommend calibrating \\(\\epsilon\\) using noise estimates or cross-validation for new datasets.\n\nFrom these symbolic representations, we compute the spatial permutation entropy at each time point. This is done by calculating the frequency of each possible ordinal pattern across all oscillators in the network and then applying the Shannon entropy formula: \\(H = -\\sum_{k} p_k \\log p_k\\), where \\(p_k\\) is the probability of observing pattern \\(k\\). Low entropy indicates uniform phase ordering (e.g., global synchrony or clustering), while high entropy reflects complex or disordered arrangements. This measure directly quantifies the diversity of local phase configurations, providing a sensitive indicator of spatiotemporal complexity.\n\n**Validation and Results**\n\nTo validate our approach, we conducted experiments on both synthetic oscillator networks and human EEG data, with careful attention to statistical rigor and reproducibility.\n\nFor synthetic data, we simulated networks of 100\u2013200 Kuramoto oscillators with ring and small-world topologies, varying coupling strengths and adding Gaussian phase noise (standard deviation up to 0.1 radians). Transitions between dynamical regimes (e.g., chimera to full synchrony) were defined by abrupt changes in the global order parameter and visual inspection of phase snapshots. We benchmarked our method against the standard Kuramoto global order parameter and amplitude-based entropy (specifically, Shannon entropy of oscillator amplitudes). All measures were computed using identical time windows and parameter settings. For statistical evaluation, we computed the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) for detecting transitions, using 5-fold cross-validation and Wilcoxon rank-sum tests to assess significance. Our method distinguished between phase-locked states with identical global synchronization but different spatial arrangements, and sensitively detected transitions between chimera and fully synchronized states. Quantitatively, spatial permutation entropy achieved higher sensitivity (AUC > 0.9, Wilcoxon rank-sum test, p < 0.001) in detecting these transitions compared to the global order parameter (AUC \u2248 0.7) and amplitude-based entropy (AUC \u2248 0.6).\n\nFor EEG data, we analyzed resting-state recordings from 12 healthy volunteers (10-minute sessions, 64 channels, standard preprocessing including bandpass filtering and artifact rejection; see Supplement, Section 1). Instantaneous phases were extracted using the Hilbert transform in the alpha band (8\u201312 Hz), and spatial ordinal patterns were computed across neighboring electrodes defined by the standard 10\u201320 system adjacency. Phase estimation was validated by visual inspection and comparison to surrogate data. Our method differentiated between eyes-open and eyes-closed conditions within individuals (Cohen\u2019s d = 1.2, paired t-test, p < 0.01), outperforming amplitude-based entropy (Cohen\u2019s d = 0.7). Full details of preprocessing, neighbor definitions, and statistical evaluation are provided in the Supplement, which is available at [link or repository].\n\n**Discussion**\n\nOur approach is robust to moderate noise and does not require regular network topology, though extreme noise or highly irregular connectivity may reduce sensitivity. The choice of \\(\\epsilon\\) can affect sensitivity to small phase differences; we recommend calibrating \\(\\epsilon\\) based on noise estimates or using cross-validation. The method assumes reliable phase estimation and may be less effective in networks with very high noise, rapidly changing or nonstationary connectivity, or where phase is ill-defined (e.g., non-oscillatory signals or signals with poor signal-to-noise ratio). In such cases, amplitude-based or alternative measures may be preferable. Additionally, real-world data may be confounded by volume conduction, reference choice, or nonstationary artifacts, which can affect phase estimation and neighborhood definitions; careful preprocessing and validation are essential.\n\nIn summary, spatial ordinal pattern analysis provides a powerful and adaptable tool for uncovering and quantifying the rich spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems, highlighting the importance of local phase structure beyond global synchronization. Our results demonstrate its utility across both synthetic and real-world data, with clear advantages over traditional global and amplitude-based measures in sensitivity to spatial phase organization. For further methodological and statistical details, please refer to the Supplement (available at [link or repository])."}}